pg:CO1 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Explain what an .xlsx workbook file is, how openpyxl represents it as a workbook, sheets and cells, and which limits of the library and its documentation apply"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can explain what an .xlsx workbook file is, how openpyxl represents it as a workbook, sheets and cells, and which limits of the library and its documentation apply, at the understand level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter14.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Understand  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO3> .
pg:CO2 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Read cells, rows, columns and ranges with names, numbers and column letters, and predict what max_row, max_column and reading an unused cell do"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can read cells, rows, columns and ranges with names, numbers and column letters, and predict what max_row, max_column and reading an unused cell do, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter14.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO3> .
pg:CO3 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Create, rename, add and remove sheets, write values, style fonts and save a workbook without overwriting the original"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can create, rename, add and remove sheets, write values, style fonts and save a workbook without overwriting the original, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter14.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO3> .
pg:CO4 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Set row heights, column widths, merges and freeze panes, write formulas, read their cached results with data_only, and build a chart from a Reference"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can set row heights, column widths, merges and freeze panes, write formulas, read their cached results with data_only, and build a chart from a Reference, at the apply level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter14.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Apply  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO3> .
pg:CO5 a ipo:LearningObjective, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Analyze where the chapter's account differs from openpyxl 3.1.5 - font defaults, Reference arguments, formulas that are not calculated or moved, dates and timezones, optimised modes and XML safety"@en ;
    skos:definition "The objective is met when the reader can analyze where the chapter's account differs from openpyxl 3.1.5 - font defaults, Reference arguments, formulas that are not calculated or moved, dates and timezones, optimised modes and XML safety, at the analyze level."@en ; dcterms:source <https://automatetheboringstuff.com/3e/chapter14.html> ;
    ld:bloomLevel ld:Analyze  ; sen0414:servesOutcome <http://example.org/sen0414#LO3> .
pg:Q1 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What is an .xlsx file, as openpyxl reads it?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q1_O0, pg:Q1_O1, pg:Q1_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q1_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The file is a zip archive; a saved workbook lists members such as xl/workbook.xml and xl/styles.xml." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A CSV file holds no sheets, fonts or formulas; an .xlsx file is a zip archive of XML parts." .
pg:Q1_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A zip archive of XML parts" .
pg:Q1_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A binary dump of Excel's memory" .
pg:Q1_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "A text file of comma-separated values" .
pg:Q2 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A new workbook is made with wb = openpyxl.Workbook(). What is wb.sheetnames before anything else is done?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q2_O0, pg:Q2_O1, pg:Q2_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q2_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Understand ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO1 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A new workbook already has one sheet, so the list is not empty." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The first sheet of a new workbook is named Sheet; Sheet1 is the name Excel gives." .
pg:Q2_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "['Sheet']" .
pg:Q2_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "[]" .
pg:Q2_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "['Sheet1']" .
pg:Q3 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does get_column_letter(27) return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q3_O0, pg:Q3_O1, pg:Q3_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q3_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Column letters do not carry a number; after Z the names are AA, AB and so on." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "AB is column 28." .
pg:Q3_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'AA'" .
pg:Q3_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'Z1'" .
pg:Q3_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'AB'" .
pg:Q4 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which cell is sheet.cell(row=3, column=2)?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q4_O0, pg:Q4_O1, pg:Q4_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q4_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The row comes first in the call, so row 3 and column 2 is B3, not C2." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That would be row 2." .
pg:Q4_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "B3" .
pg:Q4_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "C2" .
pg:Q4_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "B2" .
pg:Q5 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does openpyxl.utils.column_index_from_string('AA') return?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q5_O0, pg:Q5_O1, pg:Q5_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q5_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO2 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Column 26 is Z; AA follows it." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "52 is the index of AZ, not AA." .
pg:Q5_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "27" .
pg:Q5_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "26" .
pg:Q5_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "52" .
pg:Q6 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "After wb = openpyxl.Workbook() and wb.create_sheet(index=0, title='First'), what is wb.sheetnames?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q6_O0, pg:Q6_O1, pg:Q6_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q6_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The index 0 puts the new sheet first." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The new workbook's own sheet named Sheet is still there." .
pg:Q6_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "['First', 'Sheet']" .
pg:Q6_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "['Sheet', 'First']" .
pg:Q6_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "['First']" .
pg:Q7 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Font(size=24, italic=True) is made and assigned to a cell. What is the name attribute of that Font object?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q7_O0, pg:Q7_O1, pg:Q7_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q7_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO3 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That name appears only when it is passed to Font." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A new cell's own font is Calibri, but a Font made with other keywords has no name." .
pg:Q7_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "None" .
pg:Q7_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'Times New Roman'" .
pg:Q7_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'Calibri'" .
pg:Q8 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "A formula =SUM(A1:A2) is written with openpyxl and the file is never opened in Excel. What does load_workbook(file, data_only=True).active['A3'].value give?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q8_O0, pg:Q8_O1, pg:Q8_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q8_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "No spreadsheet application has calculated it, so there is no stored value and openpyxl does not calculate." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That is what loading without data_only gives." .
pg:Q8_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "None" .
pg:Q8_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "500" .
pg:Q8_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "'=SUM(A1:A2)'" .
pg:Q9 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "What does sheet.freeze_panes = 'C2' keep in view while the sheet scrolls?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q9_O0, pg:Q9_O1, pg:Q9_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q9_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Apply ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO4 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The cell itself is not frozen: the rows above it and the columns to its left are." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Columns to the left are frozen as well." .
pg:Q9_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Row 1 and columns A and B" .
pg:Q9_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Column C and row 2" .
pg:Q9_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Rows 1 and 2 only" .
pg:Q10 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Which block does openpyxl.chart.Reference(sheet, 2, 3, 4, 5) cover?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q10_O0, pg:Q10_O1, pg:Q10_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q10_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "That reads the integers as row first; the documented order is min_col, min_row, max_col, max_row." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "The columns are right but the rows are 3 to 5." .
pg:Q10_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "B3 to D5" .
pg:Q10_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "C2 to E4" .
pg:Q10_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "B2 to D4" .
pg:Q11 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "After sheet.merge_cells('A1:D3'), what happens on sheet['B2'] = 'x'?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q11_O0, pg:Q11_O1, pg:Q11_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q11_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "Only the top-left cell holds the value of a merged area." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "A cell inside a merged area cannot be written to." .
pg:Q11_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "An AttributeError is raised, because B2 is a read-only MergedCell" .
pg:Q11_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "The merged cell's text becomes x" .
pg:Q11_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "x is stored in B2 and A1 keeps its text" .
pg:Q12 a ld:AssessmentItem, owl:NamedIndividual ; rdfs:label "Some code reads sheet['A100'].value on a sheet whose data ends at row 7. Why does range(2, sheet.max_row + 1) then reach row 100?"@en ; ld:hasOption pg:Q12_O0, pg:Q12_O1, pg:Q12_O2 ; ld:hasCorrectOption pg:Q12_O0 ; ld:cognitiveLevel ld:Analyze ; ld:assessesObjective pg:CO5 ;
    ld:distractorRationale "In the run, max_row grew to 100 after only a read." ;
    ld:distractorRationale "range() stops at the number it is given; max_row decides that number." .
pg:Q12_O0 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "Reading a cell creates it, so max_row becomes 100" .
pg:Q12_O1 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "max_row is fixed when the file is loaded" .
pg:Q12_O2 a ld:AnswerOption ; rdfs:label "range() always runs to the last row Excel allows" .
pg:Workbooks a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Workbooks"@en ;
    skos:definition "Chapter 14 begins from three definitions that everything else in it rests on: a spreadsheet document is a workbook saved in a file with the .xlsx extension, a workbook holds sheets, and the sheet the user was looking at when the file was saved is the active sheet (Sweigart, 2025). The openpyxl module works on the file and not on the Excel application, so a program must read and save files while the application has them closed (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_Workbooks> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Workbooks" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Workbooks .
pg:WorkbookObjects a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Workbook objects"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first thing every program of the chapter does is open a workbook and find a sheet in it: openpyxl.load_workbook returns a Workbook object that stands for the file, the sheetnames attribute lists the names of its sheets, the active attribute gives the sheet that was on top when the file was saved, and square brackets with a name give any sheet (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_WorkbookObjects> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WorkbookObjects" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Workbooks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WorkbookObjects .
pg:LoadWorkbook a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Load workbook"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that once openpyxl is imported, .xlsx files are opened with openpyxl.load_workbook(), which takes the file name and returns a value of the Workbook data type that represents the Excel file, a bit like a File object represents an opened text file (Sweigart, 2025). The call reads the whole file into memory unless a mode is chosen (see Read only mode) (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_LoadWorkbook> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LoadWorkbook" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkbookObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LoadWorkbook .
pg:SheetNames a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sheet names"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the list of all the sheet names of a workbook is the sheetnames attribute, and shows ['Sheet1', 'Sheet2', 'Sheet3'] for example3.xlsx (Sweigart, 2025). The list follows the order of the tabs at the bottom of the window, which Figure 14-1 shows."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SheetNames> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SheetNames" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkbookObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SheetNames .
pg:ActiveSheet a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Active sheet"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter defines the active sheet as the sheet the user is viewing, or last viewed before closing Excel, and says that the active attribute of a Workbook is the sheet that is on top when the workbook is opened in Excel (Sweigart, 2025). Its examples take the first sheet this way."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ActiveSheet> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ActiveSheet" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkbookObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ActiveSheet .
pg:SheetByName a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sheet by name"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that each sheet is a Worksheet object that is got with square brackets and the sheet name string, like a dictionary key, and that the title attribute gives the name back (Sweigart, 2025). The old method get_sheet_by_name is not in the text of the chapter, but the script shipped with the book's files uses it (see Shipped script)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SheetByName> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SheetByName" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:WorkbookObjects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SheetByName .
pg:SheetManagement a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sheet management"@en ;
    skos:definition "A program may build a workbook as well as read one: the create_sheet method adds a sheet named SheetX at the end or, with the index and title keyword arguments, at a place of your choice, and the del operator removes a sheet like a key from a dictionary (Sweigart, 2025). A new Workbook starts with one sheet named Sheet, whose title attribute can be replaced (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SheetManagement> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SheetManagement" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Workbooks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SheetManagement .
pg:CreateSheet a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Create sheet"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that create_sheet() returns a new Worksheet object named SheetX, which by default is the last sheet in the workbook, and that the index and title keyword arguments choose its place and name (Sweigart, 2025). Its session builds ['First Sheet', 'Sheet', 'Middle Sheet', 'Sheet1']."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CreateSheet> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CreateSheet" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SheetManagement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CreateSheet .
pg:DeleteSheet a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Delete sheet"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a sheet is removed with the del operator, just like a key-value pair of a dictionary, and reminds the reader to call save() to keep the change (Sweigart, 2025). In its session, del wb['Middle Sheet'] and del wb['Sheet1'] leave ['First Sheet', 'Sheet']."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_DeleteSheet> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DeleteSheet" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SheetManagement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DeleteSheet .
pg:SheetTitleRules a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sheet title rules"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows that the title attribute of a sheet can be set, to 'Spam Bacon Eggs Sheet' in its session, and says nothing about what a title may be (Sweigart, 2025). The library applies rules when a sheet is created or renamed (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SheetTitleRules> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SheetTitleRules" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SheetManagement .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SheetTitleRules .
pg:FileHandling a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "File handling"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tells the reader to call save with a file name and to use a different name from the original, so that a bug in the program cannot damage the only copy, and it adds that save does not work while the file is open in the Excel desktop application (Sweigart, 2025). The call writes a file; what openpyxl will read back is a question of the file's name and its mode (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FileHandling> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FileHandling" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Workbooks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FileHandling .
pg:SaveWorkbook a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Save workbook"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the workbook is not saved until the save() method is called with a file name, that a different name from the original saves a copy, and that the original should be kept in case a bug writes wrong or corrupted data; it adds that save() does not work while the file is open in the Excel desktop application (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SaveWorkbook> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SaveWorkbook" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileHandling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SaveWorkbook .
pg:FileFormats a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "File formats"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a workbook is saved in a file with the .xlsx extension, and its programs open names ending in .xlsx only (Sweigart, 2025). The format is Office Open XML, a zip of XML parts standardised as ECMA 376 and ISO/IEC 29500 (Library of Congress, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FileFormats> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FileFormats" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileHandling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FileFormats .
pg:ReadOnlyMode a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Read only mode"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter loads whole workbooks and does not mention a way to open a large one cheaply, but the openpyxl documentation describes read-only and write-only modes: with read_only=True a workbook is opened for streaming, using little memory, and its cells cannot be modified (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026). Project 9 reads 72,865 rows, which is large enough for the difference to show."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ReadOnlyMode> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReadOnlyMode" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FileHandling .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReadOnlyMode .
pg:LibraryEcosystem a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Library ecosystem"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter states that Python does not come with openpyxl, so it must be installed with pip, and sends the reader to the library's documentation (Sweigart, 2025). The library is a package of the Python Package Index with one release line and one dependency, it parses files it did not write, and it is one of several modules that handle spreadsheets (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_LibraryEcosystem> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LibraryEcosystem" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Workbooks .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LibraryEcosystem .
pg:OpenpyxlRelease a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Openpyxl release"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that Python does not come with openpyxl, so it has to be installed with pip, as Appendix A explains, and that the full documentation is at openpyxl.readthedocs.io (Sweigart, 2025). It names no version."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_OpenpyxlRelease> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OpenpyxlRelease" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LibraryEcosystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OpenpyxlRelease .
pg:UntrustedFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Untrusted files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter opens spreadsheets that the reader downloads and does not say that a spreadsheet can be an attack. The openpyxl documentation does: it states that openpyxl does not guard against quadratic blowup or billion laughs XML attacks and advises installing defusedxml (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_UntrustedFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UntrustedFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LibraryEcosystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UntrustedFiles .
pg:OtherLibraries a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Other libraries"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter teaches one module, openpyxl, and sends the reader on to Google Sheets in chapter 15 (Sweigart, 2025). Other Python modules handle spreadsheets with other aims: XlsxWriter, xlrd and pandas."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_OtherLibraries> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-OtherLibraries" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:LibraryEcosystem .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:OtherLibraries .
pg:Cells a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cells"@en ;
    skos:definition "The grid of a sheet is its rows, addressed by numbers from 1, its columns, addressed by letters from A, and the cells at their crossings, each of which can hold a number or a text value (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter reads cells by name and by number, converts between column letters and numbers, reads whole rows and columns by slicing, and says that dates come back as datetime values (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_Cells> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Cells" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Cells .
pg:CellAccess a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cell access"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter gives two ways to name a cell: sheet['A1'] with the column letter and the row number, and sheet.cell(row=1, column=2) with two integers that start at 1, which suits a loop (Sweigart, 2025). A Cell object has the attributes value, row, column and coordinate, and the chapter notes that column B is the integer 2 and not the letter (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CellAccess> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CellAccess" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Cells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CellAccess .
pg:CellByName a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cell by name"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a Cell object is got from a Worksheet by its name, sheet['A1'], that its value attribute holds the value stored in the cell, and that the row, column and coordinate attributes give its place: B1 holds the string 'Apples', its row is 1, its column 2 and its coordinate 'B1' (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter adds that dates in column A come back as datetime values."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CellByName> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CellByName" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CellAccess .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CellByName .
pg:CellByNumbers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cell by numbers"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that after column Z the letters get hard to program, so a cell can also be got with the sheet's cell() method and the keyword arguments row and column, whose first value is 1 and not 0, and that the method takes the integer 2 and not the string 'B' (Sweigart, 2025). Its loop prints the values of every second row of column B."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CellByNumbers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CellByNumbers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CellAccess .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CellByNumbers .
pg:CellCreation a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cell creation"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not say that looking at a cell has an effect. In openpyxl a worksheet starts with no cells and creates a Cell object the first time one is asked for, by name or by cell(), even when only the value is read (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CellCreation> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CellCreation" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CellAccess .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CellCreation .
pg:SheetExtent a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Sheet extent"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the size of a sheet is given by the Worksheet attributes max_row and max_column, that both are integers, and that max_column is a number and not the letter shown in Excel (Sweigart, 2025). Project 9 and Project 10 both use max_row as the end of their loops (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SheetExtent> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SheetExtent" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Cells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SheetExtent .
pg:MaxRowColumn a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Max row column"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the size of a sheet is given by the max_row and max_column attributes of the Worksheet object, and that max_column is an integer rather than the letter that appears in Excel (Sweigart, 2025). For example3.xlsx they are 7 and 3 (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_MaxRowColumn> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MaxRowColumn" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SheetExtent .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MaxRowColumn .
pg:PhantomRows a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Phantom rows"@en ;
    skos:definition "The loops of Project 9 and Project 10 run from row 2 to sheet.max_row + 1, trusting that max_row is the last row that holds data (Sweigart, 2025). In openpyxl max_row is the highest row of any cell that exists, and a cell exists once it has been asked for (see Cell creation) (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_PhantomRows> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PhantomRows" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:SheetExtent .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PhantomRows .
pg:ColumnLetters a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Column letters"@en ;
    skos:definition "After column Z the letters double, AA, AB, AC, so the chapter offers two functions of openpyxl.utils: get_column_letter turns an integer such as 27 into letters and column_index_from_string turns letters back into the integer, and neither needs a workbook (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter's session shows them on small numbers."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ColumnLetters> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColumnLetters" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Cells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColumnLetters .
pg:ColumnToLetter a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Column to letter"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that to convert from numbers to letters a program calls openpyxl.utils.get_column_letter(), and that get_column_letter(27) gives the letter name of the 27th column (Sweigart, 2025). Its session prints 'A', 'B' and 'AA' for 1, 2 and 27, and 'AHP' for 900."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ColumnToLetter> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColumnToLetter" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ColumnLetters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColumnToLetter .
pg:LetterToColumn a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Letter to column"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that column_index_from_string() does the reverse of get_column_letter(): the letter name of a column goes in and its number comes out, and no workbook has to be loaded (Sweigart, 2025). Its session shows 'A' giving 1 and 'AA' giving 27."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_LetterToColumn> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-LetterToColumn" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ColumnLetters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:LetterToColumn .
pg:ColumnLimits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Column limits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter does not give the number of columns of a sheet. Excel's own limit is 16,384 columns, the last of which is XFD, and 1,048,576 rows (Microsoft Support, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ColumnLimits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColumnLimits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ColumnLetters .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColumnLimits .
pg:RangeAccess a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Range access"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter slices a Worksheet to get the Cell objects of a rectangle, for example sheet['A1':'C3'], and says the result is a tuple of tuples, one inner tuple per row; it also uses the rows and columns attributes, which must be passed to list() before an index is used, and so gets the tuple of column B as list(sheet.columns)[1] (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_RangeAccess> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RangeAccess" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Cells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RangeAccess .
pg:SliceRange a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Slice range"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter slices a Worksheet with two cell names, sheet['A1':'C3'], and says the result is a tuple that contains one inner tuple for each row of the rectangle, from the top to the bottom, each holding the Cell objects of that row from left to right (Sweigart, 2025). Two nested for loops then print the values."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SliceRange> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SliceRange" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeAccess .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SliceRange .
pg:RowsAndColumns a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rows and columns"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a Worksheet's rows and columns attributes must be converted to lists with the list() function before square brackets and an index are used, and that list(sheet.columns)[1] is the tuple of the cells of column B (Sweigart, 2025). For example3.xlsx there are seven rows of three cells and three columns of seven cells."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_RowsAndColumns> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowsAndColumns" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeAccess .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowsAndColumns .
pg:IterRows a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Iter rows"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter reads cells with slices, with rows and columns and with cell(); it does not use the iter_rows method of the worksheet, which walks the rows between limits you choose (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026). With values_only=True it hands out the values and not the Cell objects, and it is also the way to read in read-only mode (see Read only mode)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_IterRows> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-IterRows" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RangeAccess .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:IterRows .
pg:CellValues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cell values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that each cell can contain a number or text value, that openpyxl interprets the dates in column A of example3.xlsx and returns them as datetime values rather than strings, and that a formula is a value that begins with an equal sign (Sweigart, 2025). What a cell can hold is a short list of types, each with a one-letter code in the data_type attribute (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CellValues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CellValues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Cells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CellValues .
pg:DataTypes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Data types"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that each cell can contain a number or text value, and that a formula is stored by assigning text that starts with an equal sign (Sweigart, 2025). openpyxl names the type of a cell with the one-letter data_type attribute: n for a number, s for a string, b for a Boolean, d for a date and f for a formula (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_DataTypes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DataTypes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CellValues .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DataTypes .
pg:DatesAsDatetime a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dates as datetime"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the openpyxl module automatically interprets the dates in column A and returns them as datetime values rather than strings, and sends the reader to chapter 19 for the datetime type (Sweigart, 2025). A date in a spreadsheet is a number with a date format, so the type depends on the format (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_DatesAsDatetime> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DatesAsDatetime" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CellValues .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DatesAsDatetime .
pg:Projects a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Projects"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter puts the reading and writing calls to work in two projects on real files: Project 9 tallies the population and the number of census tracts of every county from a spreadsheet of 2010 census data, and Project 10 corrects the prices of garlic, celery and lemons in a spreadsheet of produce sales (Sweigart, 2025). Two practice programs close the chapter: a multiplication table maker and a blank row inserter (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_Projects> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Projects" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Projects .
pg:CensusProject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Census project"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 9 reads censuspopdata.xlsx, whose one sheet holds a row for every census tract with the tract number, state abbreviation, county name and population in columns A to D, and counts the tracts and adds the population for each county into a dictionary of dictionaries, which pprint.pformat then writes into a file census2010.py that a later program can import (Sweigart, 2025). A census tract is a geographic area defined for the purposes of the census (United States Census Bureau, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CensusProject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CensusProject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Projects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CensusProject .
pg:NestedTally a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Nested tally"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter plans the data structure of Project 9 before it writes the loop: county_data is a dictionary whose keys are the state abbreviations, each mapping to a dictionary whose keys are county names, each of which maps to a dictionary with only the two keys 'tracts' and 'pop', and for every row of the spreadsheet the loop adds one to the tracts and the tract's population to pop (Sweigart, 2025). The listing reads the rows from 2 to sheet.max_row."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_NestedTally> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-NestedTally" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CensusProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:NestedTally .
pg:SetdefaultCall a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Setdefault call"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that county_data['AK']['Anchorage']['tracts'] += 1 causes an error if the 'AK' key does not exist yet, so the loop calls setdefault() on the state, on the county inside it and with the two keys 'tracts' and 'pop' starting at 0, and that because setdefault() does nothing if the key already exists, it can be called on every iteration (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SetdefaultCall> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SetdefaultCall" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CensusProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SetdefaultCall .
pg:ResultFile a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Result file"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes county_data into census2010.py with pprint.pformat(), and says the function produces a string that is itself formatted as valid Python code, so the program has generated a Python program (Sweigart, 2025). Its listing writes the text after 'allData = '."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ResultFile> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ResultFile" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CensusProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ResultFile .
pg:ImportResult a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Import result"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that census2010.py can be imported like any other module once the shell's current working directory is the folder that holds it, and that the program that made it is throwaway code, since import census2010 is all that is needed from then on (Sweigart, 2025). It prints census2010.allData['AK']['Anchorage'] in the shell."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ImportResult> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ImportResult" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CensusProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ImportResult .
pg:ShippedScript a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Shipped script"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter tells the reader to type Project 9 in, and also says that the complete program can be downloaded from the book's online materials (Sweigart, 2025). The online materials of the 3rd edition are a zip file from No Starch Press that holds example3.xlsx, censuspopdata.xlsx, produceSales3.xlsx and the three scripts readCensusExcel.py, updateProduce.py and census2010.py."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ShippedScript> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ShippedScript" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CensusProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ShippedScript .
pg:ProduceProject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Produce project"@en ;
    skos:definition "Project 10 reads produceSales3.xlsx, whose columns are the produce, the cost per pound, the pounds sold and a TOTAL formula, and corrects the price of garlic, celery and lemons in every row from a dictionary, saving the result under a new name so that the original survives (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter says that the TOTAL cells update themselves when the cost or pounds change (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ProduceProject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ProduceProject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Projects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ProduceProject .
pg:PriceDictionary a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Price dictionary"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter first shows the corrections hard-coded as if statements and then replaces them with a dictionary, PRICE_UPDATES = {'Garlic': 3.07, 'Celery': 1.19, 'Lemon': 1.27}, saying that hardcoding is inelegant, that every change of code risks a bug, and that after the change only the dictionary needs to be edited (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_PriceDictionary> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PriceDictionary" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ProduceProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PriceDictionary .
pg:UpdateLoop a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Update loop"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter loops through the rows starting at row 2 because row 1 is the header, stores the cell of column 1 in produce_name, checks whether it is a key of PRICE_UPDATES and writes the new price into column 2, and then saves to updatedProduceSales3.xlsx so that the original is not overwritten (Sweigart, 2025). The loop ends at sheet.max_row + 1."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_UpdateLoop> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UpdateLoop" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ProduceProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UpdateLoop .
pg:CachedValueLoss a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cached value loss"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that the TOTAL column holds a formula such as =ROUND(B2*C2, 2) and that the cells in it update themselves when the cost or the pounds change (Sweigart, 2025). That is the work of the spreadsheet application."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CachedValueLoss> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CachedValueLoss" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ProduceProject .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CachedValueLoss .
pg:PracticePrograms a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Practice programs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter ends with two programs for the reader to write: a multiplication table maker, which takes a number N from the command line and writes an N by N table to a sheet with the labels in bold, and a blank row inserter, which takes two numbers and a file name and inserts blank rows into a spreadsheet (Sweigart, 2025). Both use the command line."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_PracticePrograms> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-PracticePrograms" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Projects .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:MultiplicationTable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Multiplication table"@en ;
    skos:definition "The first practice program of the chapter takes a number N from the command line and creates an N by N multiplication table in an Excel spreadsheet, with the labels in bold: for N=6, the table's labels are 1 to 6 along row 1 from B1 and down column A from A2, with the products inside (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_MultiplicationTable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MultiplicationTable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MultiplicationTable .
pg:BlankRowInserter a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Blank row inserter"@en ;
    skos:definition "The second practice program takes two integers and a file name from the command line, N and M, and inserts M blank rows into the spreadsheet starting at row N, saving the result (Sweigart, 2025). openpyxl has a method for the job: insert_rows(idx, amount) inserts amount rows before row idx (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_BlankRowInserter> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-BlankRowInserter" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:BlankRowInserter .
pg:InsertRowsLimits a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Insert rows limits"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's practice program asks only that the rows be inserted, and the one call, insert_rows, does that for values (Sweigart, 2025). Everything in a sheet that refers to a row by its number is another matter, and the library moves the cells without correcting such references (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_InsertRowsLimits> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-InsertRowsLimits" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:InsertRowsLimits .
pg:CommandLineNumbers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Command line numbers"@en ;
    skos:definition "Both practice programs take their numbers from the command line (Sweigart, 2025). sys.argv is the list of the arguments as strings, with the name of the script first, so the numbers have to be turned into integers with int() (Python Software Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CommandLineNumbers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CommandLineNumbers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:PracticePrograms .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CommandLineNumbers .
pg:Formulas a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Formulas"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter explains that a formula is written into a cell like any text value, begins with an equal sign, and is shown by Excel as its result; it adds that openpyxl cannot calculate formulas, so the file must be opened and saved in Excel before data_only=True can read the result (Sweigart, 2025). The TOTAL column of the produce file is a formula, =ROUND(B2*C2, 2), in every row (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_Formulas> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Formulas" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Formulas .
pg:FormulaStorage a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Formula storage"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that Excel formulas begin with an equal sign and can configure cells to contain values calculated from other cells, and that a program adds one just as it adds any normal value, with sheet['B9'] = '=SUM(B1:B8)' (Sweigart, 2025). Formulas are a form of programming that can quickly become unmanageable, as the chapter's long VLOOKUP example shows."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FormulaStorage> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FormulaStorage" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Formulas .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FormulaStorage .
pg:FormulaString a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Formula string"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's session sets A1 to 200 and A2 to 300 and A3 to '=SUM(A1:A2)', and says that when the spreadsheet is opened in Excel A3 displays 500; the openpyxl module doesn't have the ability to calculate Excel formulas and populate cells with the results (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FormulaString> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FormulaString" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormulaStorage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FormulaString .
pg:ReadFormulaBack a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Read formula back"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a workbook loaded as usual gives the formula and that, after the file has been opened and saved in Excel, loading it with data_only=True makes the cell values show the calculation results instead of the formula string (Sweigart, 2025). Its printed values are '=SUM(A1:A2)' and then 500."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ReadFormulaBack> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReadFormulaBack" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FormulaStorage .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReadFormulaBack .
pg:CachedResults a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Cached results"@en ;
    skos:definition "A formula cell of an .xlsx file holds two things, the formula and the result that the last program to calculate it stored (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026). The chapter's reading of results with data_only=True reads the second, and the produce file's TOTAL column is the chapter's case (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_CachedResults> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-CachedResults" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Formulas .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:CachedResults .
pg:DataOnly a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Data only"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter passes data_only=True to openpyxl.load_workbook() so that cell values show the calculation results instead of the formula string, and says the 500 appears only if the file was opened and saved in Excel (Sweigart, 2025). The openpyxl documentation describes the flag as choosing between the formula and the value stored at the last save by a spreadsheet application (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_DataOnly> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DataOnly" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CachedResults .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DataOnly .
pg:RecalcByOffice a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Recalc by office"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's step is to open the file in Excel and save it, so that Excel populates the cells with the formula results (Sweigart, 2025). The chapter notes that LibreOffice is a free alternative that reads and writes .xlsx files, and a command line option of LibreOffice, --headless with --convert-to, does the same without a window (The Document Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_RecalcByOffice> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RecalcByOffice" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CachedResults .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RecalcByOffice .
pg:WriterCachedValue a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Writer cached value"@en ;
    skos:definition "Another module, XlsxWriter, creates .xlsx files and can write formulas, which the chapter does not mention (XlsxWriter documentation, 2026). A writer that does not calculate must put something in the place where the result is kept."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_WriterCachedValue> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-WriterCachedValue" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CachedResults .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:WriterCachedValue .
pg:RoundingDiffers a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rounding differs"@en ;
    skos:definition "The produce file's TOTAL is =ROUND(B2*C2, 2), which the chapter explains as multiplying the cost per pound by the pounds sold and rounding to the nearest cent (Sweigart, 2025). A program that checks the totals would write round(b * c, 2) in Python, and the two are not the same (Microsoft Support, 2026) (The Document Foundation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_RoundingDiffers> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RoundingDiffers" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:CachedResults .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RoundingDiffers .
pg:Formatting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Formatting"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows how a program changes the look of a sheet: the Font function sets the name, size, bold and italic of a cell, row_dimensions and column_dimensions set heights and widths, merge_cells and unmerge_cells join and split rectangles of cells, and freeze_panes keeps the top rows and left columns on screen while the rest scrolls (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_Formatting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Formatting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Formatting .
pg:FontStyle a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Font style"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter shows that styling certain cells helps to emphasise important areas, and that to customise a cell's font a program imports Font from openpyxl.styles, makes a Font object with keyword arguments and assigns it to the font attribute of a cell (Sweigart, 2025). Table 14-2 lists the keyword arguments name, size, bold and italic."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FontStyle> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FontStyle" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Formatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FontStyle .
pg:FontObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Font object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter creates a new workbook, makes italic_24_font = Font(size=24, italic=True), assigns it to sheet['A1'].font and then sets sheet['A1'] = 'Hello, world!', saying that the keyword arguments configure the Font object's styling information (Sweigart, 2025). The text is set after the font and keeps it."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FontObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FontObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FontStyle .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FontObject .
pg:FontKeywords a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Font keywords"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's Table 14-2 gives four keyword arguments for Font(): name, a string such as 'Calibri' or 'Times New Roman'; size, an integer, the point size; bold, a Boolean; and italic, a Boolean (Sweigart, 2025). It does not list the short forms or say what happens with a wrong value."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FontKeywords> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FontKeywords" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FontStyle .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FontKeywords .
pg:FontDefaults a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Font defaults"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says of A1, whose font name is Times New Roman and bold is true, that no point size was given so the text uses the openpyxl default, 11, and of B3, an italic 24-point font, that no name was given so the text uses the openpyxl default, Calibri (Sweigart, 2025). A reader takes from this that a Font fills what is left out."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FontDefaults> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FontDefaults" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FontStyle .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FontDefaults .
pg:StyleImmutable a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Style immutable"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter assigns a Font object to a cell and never changes one afterwards, which agrees with the openpyxl documentation: style objects are shared between cells and, once assigned, cannot be changed (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026). To change a style, make a new object, possibly as a copy, and assign it."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_StyleImmutable> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-StyleImmutable" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FontStyle .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:StyleImmutable .
pg:RowsColumnsSize a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Rows columns size"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that a Worksheet has row_dimensions and column_dimensions attributes that control row heights and column widths, that both are dictionary-like, and that rows can also be hidden or frozen in place (Sweigart, 2025). The default width and height of cells, it adds, vary between versions of Excel and openpyxl."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_RowsColumnsSize> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowsColumnsSize" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Formatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowsColumnsSize .
pg:RowHeight a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Row height"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter sets sheet.row_dimensions[1].height = 70 and says that row_dimensions contains RowDimension objects, which are reached by the row number, here 1 or 2 (Sweigart, 2025). The saved dimensions3.xlsx is Figure 14-6, whose alt text says that the text of cell B1 is Wide column."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_RowHeight> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-RowHeight" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RowsColumnsSize .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:RowHeight .
pg:ColumnWidth a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Column width"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter sets sheet.column_dimensions['B'].width = 20 and says that the objects of column_dimensions are ColumnDimension objects reached by the letter of the column, here A or B (Sweigart, 2025). The width unit is that of Excel and its limit is 255 characters (Microsoft Support, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ColumnWidth> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ColumnWidth" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RowsColumnsSize .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ColumnWidth .
pg:DimensionReadCreates a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Dimension read creates"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter treats row_dimensions and column_dimensions as dictionary-like and addresses an entry by its key. In openpyxl the holders create an entry the first time a key is asked for, as a defaultdict does, even when the program only reads the height or the width (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_DimensionReadCreates> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-DimensionReadCreates" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RowsColumnsSize .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:DimensionReadCreates .
pg:HidingRowsColumns a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Hiding rows columns"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that rows and columns can be hidden from view, and its Practice Question 16 asks how to hide a column, but the text never shows the way (Sweigart, 2025). The dimension objects have a hidden attribute that does it (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_HidingRowsColumns> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-HidingRowsColumns" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:RowsColumnsSize .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:HidingRowsColumns .
pg:MergedCells a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Merged cells"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter merges a rectangular group of cells into a single cell with merge_cells(), whose argument is a string of the top-left and bottom-right cells, so that 'A1:D3' merges 12 cells, and splits it again with unmerge_cells() (Sweigart, 2025). To set the value of merged cells, set the top-left cell."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_MergedCells> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MergedCells" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Formatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MergedCells .
pg:MergeCells a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Merge cells"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes 'Twelve cells merged together.' into A1, calls sheet.merge_cells('A1:D3'), writes 'Two merged cells.' into C5 and merges 'C5:D5', and says the argument is a single string (Sweigart, 2025). The file is called merged3.xlsx in the code and merged.xlsx in the prose."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_MergeCells> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MergeCells" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MergedCells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MergeCells .
pg:MergedValue a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Merged value"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that to set the value of the merged cells you set the value of the top-left cell of the merged group (Sweigart, 2025). The reason is in the library: in a merged range only the top-left cell is a real Cell and the others become MergedCell objects (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_MergedValue> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-MergedValue" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MergedCells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:MergedValue .
pg:UnmergeCells a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Unmerge cells"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter calls sheet.unmerge_cells('A1:D3') and 'C5:D5' and says that, after saving, the merged cells have gone back to being individual cells (Sweigart, 2025). The text stays where it was."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_UnmergeCells> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-UnmergeCells" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:MergedCells .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:UnmergeCells .
pg:FreezePanes a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Freeze panes"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that for spreadsheets too large to display at once, a program can freeze a few of the top rows or the leftmost columns, that the freeze_panes attribute of a Worksheet is set to a Cell object or a string of coordinates, and that all the rows above that cell and all the columns left of it are frozen but not its own row and column (Sweigart, 2025). Table 14-3 gives the settings."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FreezePanes> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FreezePanes" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Formatting .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FreezePanes .
pg:FreezeSetting a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Freeze setting"@en ;
    skos:definition "Table 14-3 says that freeze_panes = 'A2' freezes row 1, 'B1' freezes column A, 'C1' freezes columns A and B, and 'C2' freezes row 1 and columns A and B, always the cells above and to the left of the named cell (Sweigart, 2025). Microsoft's instructions say to select the cell below the rows and to the right of the columns that are to stay visible (Microsoft Support, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FreezeSetting> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FreezeSetting" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FreezePanes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FreezeSetting .
pg:FreezeUnfreeze a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Freeze unfreeze"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that to unfreeze all panes the attribute is set to None or 'A1', and that a Cell object can be assigned in place of its coordinates (Sweigart, 2025). The sentence is typeset 'freeze _panes', with a stray space."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FreezeUnfreeze> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FreezeUnfreeze" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FreezePanes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FreezeUnfreeze .
pg:FreezeScroll a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Freeze scroll"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter freezes row 1 of produceSales3.xlsx with sheet.freeze_panes = 'A2', saves freezeExample3.xlsx, and says in Figure 14-8 that row 1 remains visible no matter where the user scrolls (Sweigart, 2025). The figure shows the top of the data."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FreezeScroll> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FreezeScroll" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:FreezePanes .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FreezeScroll .
pg:Charts a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Charts"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's last topic is charts: openpyxl supports bar, line, scatter and pie charts made from the data in a sheet, in five steps: make a Reference object from a rectangle of cells, make a Series from it, make a chart, append the series to the chart and add the chart to the worksheet at a cell (Sweigart, 2025)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_Charts> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-Charts" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 1 .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:Charts .
pg:ChartBuilding a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart building"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter lists five steps for a chart: create a Reference object from a rectangular selection of cells, create a Series object from it, create a Chart object, append the Series to the Chart, and add the Chart to the Worksheet with add_chart, optionally at a cell (Sweigart, 2025). Its session builds a bar chart from the squares in A1 to A10."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartBuilding> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartBuilding" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Charts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartBuilding .
pg:ReferenceObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Reference object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that openpyxl.chart.Reference() takes five arguments: the worksheet, and the column and row of the top-left cell, and the column and row of the bottom-right cell; but it describes the pairs the other way round, saying that the first integer is the row and the second is the column (Sweigart, 2025). Its call is Reference(sheet, 1, 1, 1, 10)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ReferenceObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ReferenceObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartBuilding .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ReferenceObject .
pg:SeriesObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Series object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes the series with openpyxl.chart.Series(ref_obj, title='First series'), which is called with the Reference object, and appends it to the chart (Sweigart, 2025). The call is a function of the module that returns a series object."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_SeriesObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-SeriesObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartBuilding .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:SeriesObject .
pg:ChartObject a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart object"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter makes a BarChart, gives it the title 'My Chart', appends the Series and adds the chart to the sheet at 'C5' with sheet.add_chart(chart_obj, 'C5'), then saves sampleChart3.xlsx (Sweigart, 2025). The chart class decides the kind of chart."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartObject> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartObject" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartBuilding .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartObject .
pg:ChartKinds a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart kinds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter says that openpyxl supports creating bar, line, scatter and pie charts using the data in a sheet's cells (Sweigart, 2025). They are the classes BarChart, LineChart, ScatterChart and PieChart of openpyxl.chart."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartKinds> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartKinds" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Charts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartKinds .
pg:FourKinds a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Four kinds"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter names bar, line, scatter and pie charts as the four kinds that can be made from a sheet's cells (Sweigart, 2025). The documentation has pages for each of them, and for others such as area and bubble charts (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_FourKinds> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-FourKinds" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:FourKinds .
pg:ScatterValues a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Scatter values"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's chart section builds a bar chart from one column and says nothing of what a scatter chart needs, which is a pair of numbers for each point (Sweigart, 2025). In openpyxl a Series for a scatter chart takes the x values as a second reference, the xvalues argument (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ScatterValues> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ScatterValues" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartKinds .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ScatterValues .
pg:ChartFiles a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart files"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter saves its chart in sampleChart3.xlsx and shows the result as a picture drawn by Excel, Figure 14-10 and 14-11 (Sweigart, 2025). A chart in the file is XML that an application draws; the file does not hold a picture."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartFiles> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartFiles" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 2 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:Charts .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartFiles .
pg:ChartXml a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart xml"@en ;
    skos:definition "A chart is not kept in the sheet's XML but in parts of its own: the .xlsx file is a zip file, and a chart makes a drawing part that anchors it to the sheet and a chart part that describes it (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026) (Library of Congress, 2026). The chapter does not open the file."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartXml> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartXml" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartXml .
pg:ChartRoundTrip a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart round trip"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter writes a chart and never reloads the file. The tutorial of openpyxl warns that it does not read all possible items in an Excel file, so shapes will be lost from existing files if they are opened and saved with the same name (OpenPyXL documentation, 2026)."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartRoundTrip> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartRoundTrip" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartRoundTrip .
pg:ChartRendered a ipo:ChapterSection ; rdfs:label "Chart rendered"@en ;
    skos:definition "The chapter's Figure 14-10 and 14-11 show the sample chart as Excel draws it (Sweigart, 2025). How a chart looks, its colours, its axis range and its legend, is decided by the application that draws it and not by the file alone."@en ; dcterms:source <http://example.org/sen0414/ch14#S_ChartRendered> ;
    sen0414:anchor "#s-ChartRendered" ; sen0414:sectionLevel 3 ; dcterms:isPartOf pg:ChartFiles .
pg:Page ipo:coversChapterSection pg:ChartRendered .
